---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UrlEncoderDecoder from '@/components/tools/dev/UrlEncoderDecoder.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="url-encoder-decoder">
  <UrlEncoderDecoder client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to URL encode or decode</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>On the <strong>Encode / decode</strong> tab, choose a direction and the encoding rule that matches where the value will end up.</li>
      <li>Paste the text. The converted value appears next to it, with a count of the escape sequences.</li>
      <li>If decoding fails, the message gives the exact character position of the broken escape, not only the browser's "URI malformed".</li>
      <li>On the <strong>Query string</strong> tab, paste a full URL to break its parameters into an editable table, then copy the rebuilt URL.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>encodeURI or encodeURIComponent?</h2>
    <p>
      This choice causes more broken links than anything else. The two functions differ only in which
      characters they leave unchanged.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Function</th><th>Leaves unescaped</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>encodeURI</code></td>
          <td>A-Z a-z 0-9 and <code>; , / ? : @ &amp; = + $ - _ . ! ~ * ' ( ) #</code></td>
          <td>A complete URL you want to make safe without destroying its structure</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>encodeURIComponent</code></td>
          <td>A-Z a-z 0-9 and <code>- _ . ! ~ * ' ( )</code></td>
          <td>One query value, one path segment, one form field</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Form encoding</td>
          <td>A-Z a-z 0-9 and <code>- _ .</code>; space becomes <code>+</code></td>
          <td>An <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> body</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Build the URL yourself, and run <code>encodeURIComponent</code> on each value you put into it.
      <code>encodeURI</code> on a URL that already contains an unencoded value does not fix it, because
      <code>encodeURI</code> keeps <code>&amp;</code> and <code>=</code>, the two characters most likely to be
      inside the value and break the parse.
    </p>

    <h2>Common escape sequences</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Character</th><th>Escaped</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>space</td><td><code>%20</code> or <code>+</code></td><td><code>+</code> only means space in a query string, never in a path</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&amp;</code></td><td><code>%26</code></td><td>Unescaped, it starts a new parameter</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>=</code></td><td><code>%3D</code></td><td>Unescaped, it splits key from value</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>?</code></td><td><code>%3F</code></td><td>Unescaped, it starts the query string</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>#</code></td><td><code>%23</code></td><td>Everything after it is a fragment and never reaches the server</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/</code></td><td><code>%2F</code></td><td>Escape it inside a path segment that legitimately contains a slash</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>%</code></td><td><code>%25</code></td><td>A bare percent sign makes the whole URL undecodable</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>é</code></td><td><code>%C3%A9</code></td><td>Non-ASCII becomes its UTF-8 bytes, two escapes here</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why decoding sometimes fails</h2>
    <p>
      Percent encoding has two rules: a <code>%</code> must be followed by two hexadecimal digits, and the
      bytes those escapes give must be valid UTF-8. A URL cut in the middle of a sequence, often by a database
      column that is too short or a trimmed log line, breaks both. The decoder gives the position of the first
      broken escape, so you can see whether the value was cut short or was never encoded correctly.
    </p>

    <h2>Editing query parameters</h2>
    <p>
      The query-string tab decodes each parameter for display, so you edit readable text, and encodes it again
      on output. It handles repeated keys (common in filter UIs), parameters with no value and semicolon
      separators, and keeps the fragment. Use it to remove UTM parameters, change a page number, or reproduce
      a bug report's exact query with one value changed.
    </p>

    <h2>Runs in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      URLs often contain session identifiers, signed download links and internal hostnames. JavaScript in your
      own tab does the work, and no server logs it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
